{"kind":"request","text":"Design and prove the host-side wake-up seam for priority ephemeral chat, without pretending that an MCP tool server controls when an idle model is run. The current adapter already offers `wait`, but a host that does not keep a wait call or subscription active will not invoke the model merely because the nexus has a new frame. That is an orchestration boundary, not a polling-interval bug inside `say`.\n\nStart with a capability matrix for the Codex and Claude command-line/desktop hosts actually used in this workroom: whether each can keep an MCP subscription or long poll active, surface a server notification, resume an existing task, inject trusted context into that task, or only deliver tool results during a user/model turn. Check the current MCP 2026-07-28 subscription model and do not build new delivery on deprecated logging or sampling. Separate protocol transport from host policy: receipt by an MCP client is not proof that its host woke a model.\n\nUsing the recipient-aware inbox from the parent request, define one small portable watch contract carrying the exact actor, text, about anchor, conversation/sequence, reply target, and priority reason. Prefer an opt-in MCP extension/subscription when the host declares support; otherwise expose the same semantics through a documented long-running local watcher/NDJSON seam that an orchestrator can consume. Implement and end-to-end test at least one host integration if a supported wake/resume API is available in this environment. If neither host exposes such an API, the deliverable is a ratifiable design/spike artifact that proves the boundary, specifies the smallest host change required, and leaves the repository with a testable watcher seam rather than a fake notification claim.\n\nA supported bridge must resume or notify only the addressed actor, coalesce duplicate delivery across that actor's sessions, preserve the exact thread pointer, rate-limit storms, and make quiet/off state explicit. The resumed agent context must label a direct mention or reply as priority ephemeral chat, instruct it to inspect and answer the chat before routine work when appropriate, and state plainly that chat is not a durable assignment. It must never wake a different actor, impersonate the sender, make a promise automatically, or persist the conversation beyond nexus expiry. Document setup, observable delivery states, capability fallback, disconnect/reconnect, expiry, and security/trust boundaries.","body":{"branch":"request/ephemeral-host-wakeup","conditions":"A checked capability matrix covers the Codex and Claude hosts used here; the design distinguishes MCP receipt from model wake-up and avoids deprecated logging/sampling; one portable actor-targeted watch contract is specified and tested; at least one real host wake/resume integration is demonstrated when a supported API exists, otherwise a ratifiable spike precisely names the missing host capability while still delivering a testable local watcher seam; dedupe, coalescing, rate limits, quiet/off, reconnect, expiry, thread routing, and actor isolation are covered; resumed context gives priority-chat guidance without creating durable obligation; documentation states exactly which hosts are immediate, polling-only, or unsupported.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}